Change only the members named. An empty value clears one, as in --model "". The result is checked as a whole profile, so one update reaches any valid profile.

--key reads a new key as create does, into the OS keychain, or into profiles.json with --file. --provider keeps base_url, account_id and model, and names on stderr the ones it keeps. On a profile that holds a key it needs --key, since a key belongs to one provider. An account_id kept on a provider that takes none is refused, so clear it with --account-id "" in the same update.

--name renames the profile and moves a keychain item named after it, and the default follows it. An empty key typed at the prompt drops the stored key and its keychain item, when the base URL is not the provider's own. An empty key on stdin never drops a key.

Usage:
  onesie profile update NAME [flags]

Examples:
  onesie profile update work --model typesafe/jev-1.13
  pass show work | head -n 1 | onesie profile update work --key
  onesie profile update work --name personal

Flags:
      --account-id ACCOUNT_ID   The account id, 32 hexadecimal characters. Only on
                                cloudflare.
      --allow-http              Store a plain http --base-url to a host other than this
                                machine. Every run on the profile then needs
                                --allow-http too.
      --base-url URL            The API root the profile's runs use.
      --file                    Store the new key in profiles.json, not the OS keychain.
                                Needs --key.
  -h, --help                    Show this help.
      --key                     Read a new key from a prompt or stdin.
      --model MODEL             The model a run uses when it names none.
      --name NAME               A new name for the profile. A keychain item named after
                                it moves with it, and the default follows it.
      --provider PROVIDER       A new provider for the profile. On a profile that holds
                                a key it needs --key.
